        <h3>Must-Have Website Elements</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dedicated landing pages</strong> for each service — not your homepage. One contractor improved from 3.56% to 5.77% conversion, dropping cost per lead from $196 to $121</li>
          <li><strong>Click-to-call buttons</strong> — large, thumb-sized, visible without scrolling</li>
          <li><strong>Instant estimate tool</strong> above the fold with a progress indicator</li>
          <li><strong>Trust signals:</strong> BBB accreditation, manufacturer certifications (GAF, Owens Corning), state license, insurance details, years in business</li>
          <li><strong>Real testimonials</strong> with full names, locations, and before/after photos</li>
          <li><strong>Photo gallery</strong> with high-resolution project photos and drone shots</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Mobile-First Design</h3>
        <p>Over 60% of roofing searches happen on mobile. Desktop users convert around 4.1%, while mobile users convert around 1.5%. Close that gap by eliminating multi-field forms, using instant callback options, and avoiding layouts that require zooming.</p>

        <h3>Page Speed</h3>
        <p>Every 1-second delay in load time reduces conversions by approximately 7%. Target a PageSpeed Insights score of 80+. Compress images, enable caching, and minimize JavaScript.</p>

        <h2>Chapter 4: Storm Damage and Emergency Lead Generation</h2>
        <p>Storm damage leads are the highest-value, fastest-closing leads in roofing. The average ticket is $12,000–$20,000. But the window to capture these leads is <strong>24–48 hours after a storm</strong>. After that, your competitors have already knocked on their doors.</p>

        <h3>The Storm Damage Lead Opportunity</h3>
        <p>Twenty-two percent of all roofing projects originate from storm damage. In 2024, 12 million properties were hit by hail. Insurance carriers are shortening acceptable roof ages to 15–20 years, which means more claims and more replacement opportunities.</p>

        <h3>Pre-Storm Preparation Checklist</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Build storm-specific landing pages for each service area</li>
          <li>Pre-write Google Ads campaigns for emergency roofing keywords</li>
          <li>Pre-design direct mail postcards for storm-affected zip codes</li>
          <li>Set up geo-targeted Facebook ads ready to activate</li>
          <li>Train canvassing crews on hail identification</li>
          <li>Establish relationships with insurance adjusters before storm season</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Precision Targeting</h3>
        <p>Do not blanket an entire metro. Use forensic-grade storm data: <strong>HailTrace</strong> for real-time zip-code level data, <strong>NOAA/NSSL</strong> for official radar overlays, and <strong>GAF WeatherHub</strong> for storm history. Skip yellow zones. Hit red zones (1-inch+ hail).</p>

        <h3>The Insurance Adjuster Angle</h3>
        <p>Most homeowners have never filed a roof claim and are intimidated by the process. Your winning offer: <strong>"Free Roof Inspection + Adjuster Meeting Support."</strong></p>
        <p>Promise to meet the insurance adjuster on-site to ensure damage is not missed. Emphasize that you handle technical documentation and supplement negotiations. Reference RCV (Replacement Cost Value) vs. ACV (Actual Cash Value). <strong>Warning:</strong> Zero-deductible advertising is banned in Texas, Colorado, and Oklahoma.</p>

