        <h3>Weather-Triggered Marketing</h3>
        <p>Smart roofers tie marketing to weather events: 48 hours before a storm, send preparation tips. Within 6 hours after hail, activate geo-targeted ads. Within 24–48 hours, deploy door-to-door canvassers. Within 72 hours, mail postcards. One week after, publish "What to do after hail damage." Two weeks after, retarget website visitors.</p>

        <h3>Slow Season Marketing (November–February)</h3>
        <p>Most roofers go quiet in winter. That is a mistake. Slow season is when you build systems, create content, strengthen partnerships, train your team, and plan for storm season. The roofers who market consistently in slow season dominate when peak season arrives.</p>

        <h2>Roofing Marketing Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>What is the best way to market a roofing company?</h3>
        <p>The best approach is a diversified system combining local SEO, paid advertising, and referral marketing. Local SEO delivers the highest long-term ROI. Paid ads generate immediate leads. Referrals provide the highest-quality, lowest-cost leads. No single channel should contribute more than 40% of your leads.</p>

        <h3>How much should a roofing company spend on marketing?</h3>
        <p>Roofing companies should spend 5–12% of revenue on marketing, depending on size. Companies under $500K should spend 8–12%. Companies over $3M can spend 4–6%. The key metric is cost per sale and overall ROI, not just spend.</p>

        <h3>How do roofers get leads?</h3>
        <p>Roofers get leads through six primary channels: local SEO and Google Business Profile (highest ROI long-term), paid advertising like Google Ads and Facebook (fastest results), referral networks and partnerships (highest close rates), storm damage marketing (highest ticket size), content marketing (builds trust over time), and lead aggregators like HomeAdvisor (lowest quality but fills gaps).</p>

        <h3>What is the average cost per lead for roofing?</h3>
        <p>Cost per lead varies dramatically by channel. Google Ads averages $124–$228 per lead. Google Local Services Ads range from $50–$150. Facebook and Instagram ads average $30–$100. Lead aggregators charge $40–$150 for shared leads. Referrals cost $10–$25 in incentive costs. SEO leads have no per-lead cost after the initial investment.</p>

        <h3>How do I get storm damage leads?</h3>
        <p>Storm damage leads require speed and precision. Use hail tracking tools like HailTrace to identify affected neighborhoods within hours of a storm. Activate pre-built PPC campaigns, deploy door-to-door canvassers with hail swath maps, and mail postcards to affected zip codes within 72 hours. The golden window is 24–48 hours after the storm.</p>

        <h3>Should roofers use PPC or SEO?</h3>
        <p>Use both. SEO is a long-term investment that delivers free leads once you rank, but takes 3–6 months. PPC delivers immediate leads but requires ongoing budget. The ideal strategy: use PPC for immediate cash flow and storm season, while building SEO for sustainable, low-cost lead generation year-round.</p>

        <h3>How important are reviews for roofing companies?</h3>
        <p>Reviews are critical. Thirty-five percent of homeowners rely on online reviews during contractor selection. Roofers with 15+ new reviews per month earn 3x more calls. Google considers review volume, velocity, and response rate as primary ranking factors for local SEO.</p>

        <h3>What should a roofing company website include?</h3>
        <p>A roofing website needs dedicated landing pages for each service, prominent click-to-call buttons, trust signals (licenses, insurance, certifications), before/after photo galleries, real customer testimonials, an instant estimate tool, and mobile-optimized design. Optimized sites convert at 8–14.5% versus 0.5–2% for average sites.</p>

        <h3>How fast should roofers respond to leads?</h3>
        <p>Speed is critical. Contacting a lead within 1 minute improves conversion rates by up to 391%. The first contractor to respond wins the job approximately 78% of the time. Top performers use automated systems to respond in under 5 minutes.</p>

        <h3>Do financing options help roofing sales?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Offering 0% down or multiple payment plans can improve lead conversion by up to 42%. Many homeowners delay roof replacement because they cannot afford the upfront cost. Financing removes that barrier.</p>

        <h3>What is the difference between exclusive and shared leads?</h3>
        <p>Exclusive leads come directly to you through your own website, ads, or referrals. They close at 30–50%. Shared leads from aggregators are sold to 3–5 contractors simultaneously. They close below 10–15% because homeowners default to the lowest price. Always prioritize exclusive lead channels.</p>

        <h3>How long does SEO take for roofing companies?</h3>
        <p>Most roofing companies see initial ranking movement within 30–60 days, with significant Map Pack improvements in 60–90 days. Competitive top-3 positions usually require 3–6 months of sustained effort. SEO is a long-term investment — the leads it generates after month 6 cost essentially nothing.</p>

        <h3>What is the Roofing Revenue Cycle?</h3>
        <p>The Roofing Revenue Cycle is a six-stage framework: Attract → Search → Evaluate → Convert → Deliver → Amplify. The best roofing companies optimize every stage — not just lead generation. A 10% improvement in close rate is worth more than a 50% increase in lead volume if your follow-up is slow.</p>

        <h3>Should I hire a marketing agency or do it in-house?</h3>
        <p>Companies under $1M often get better results with in-house execution guided by a consultant. Companies over $2M benefit from agency expertise in PPC management, SEO, and content at scale. The hybrid approach — in-house for referrals and local SEO, agency for PPC and technical SEO — often works best.</p>

        <h3>How do I compete with storm chasers?</h3>
        <p>Storm chasers have one advantage: speed. You compete by being faster and more trustworthy. Build pre-storm landing pages and ad campaigns ready to activate. Establish insurance adjuster relationships before storm season. Emphasize your local roots, licensing, and warranty. Position yourself as the permanent local roofer who will be there for warranty claims.</p>

